{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-32569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a1b58ae05387bd3d0ec12ff43a1ecb313b907f716f951dee5fe224c7fc0d71c"} {"question" "q-32569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a1b58ae05387bd3d0ec12ff43a1ecb313b907f716f951dee5fe224c7fc0d71c"} {"question" "q-106836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a1b58ae05387bd3d0ec12ff43a1ecb313b907f716f951dee5fe224c7fc0d71c"} {"question" "q-106842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2},{u}_{33}=3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a1b58ae05387bd3d0ec12ff43a1ecb313b907f716f951dee5fe224c7fc0d71c"} {"question" "q-75093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 16 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 16 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\