t {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a1b58ae05387bd3d0ec12ff43a1ecb313b907f716f951dee5fe224c7fc0d71c"} {"question" "q-106843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a1b58ae05387bd3d0ec12ff43a1ecb313b907f716f951dee5fe224c7fc0d71c"} {"question" "q-75102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-75102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a1b58ae05387bd3d0ec12ff43a1ecb313b907f716f951dee5fe224c7fc0d71c"} {"question" "q-106855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-106855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.25){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.25 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}B & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.25 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ B & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.25 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ B & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.25 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.25,{u}_{33}=-3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-0.75.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/state.json" "file_sha256" "7df46a56da5e0fd9c01dcfd69f0747cdcdc3f87807f4bcec874eaa1e11b8d00c"} {"question" "q-32720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/activity-metadata.json" 